== A  the inverse step
PASS A1 J * J^-1 = 1
PASS A2 J^-1 = phi * conj(j)
PASS A3 J^-1 = phi^2 * conj(J)
PASS A4 J * phi = j
PASS A5 J^5 * phi^5 = 1
== B  Galois and the inverse
PASS B1 sigma_1(J) != J^-1
PASS B1 sigma_2(J) != J^-1
PASS B1 sigma_3(J) != J^-1
PASS B1 sigma_4(J) != J^-1
   a=1  J^-1 / sigma_a(J) = [-1, -1, 0, 1]  in mu_10: False
   a=2  J^-1 / sigma_a(J) = [0, 0, -1, 0]  in mu_10: True
   a=3  J^-1 / sigma_a(J) = [0, -1, 0, 0]  in mu_10: True
   a=4  J^-1 / sigma_a(J) = [1, 0, -1, -1]  in mu_10: False
PASS B2 inversion up to torsion exactly for the place swap a in {2,3}
PASS B3 J^-1 / sigma_2(J) = -j^2 = zeta_10^-1
PASS B4 J^-1 / sigma_3(J) = -j = zeta_10^-3
PASS B5 J^-1 / sigma_4(J) = phi^2 (not torsion)
PASS B6 sigma_2 o sigma_2 = sigma_4 on J
PASS B7 the only Galois element of order 2 (sigma_4) does not invert J up to torsion
PASS B8 Tr(J) = 3 and Tr(J^-1) = 2, so M_J and M_J^-1 are not conjugate in GL_4(Q)
== C  closed world: J periodic modulo rational m, ord_m(J) = lcm(5, ord_m(phi))
